# PVC Security Rules (English Canonical)

> Korean siblings: `security/auth.mdc`, `input-validation.mdc`, `secret-management.mdc`.

## 1. Authentication & Sessions

- Choose auth by threat model and clients—not fashion: server session+cookie, OAuth2.1/OIDC, short-lived JWT+refresh, or machine mTLS/API keys.
- Do not store access/refresh tokens in `localStorage`/`sessionStorage`. Prefer **HttpOnly + Secure + appropriate SameSite** cookies for browsers.
- Keep access tokens short-lived; rotate refresh tokens; revoke on logout/password/privilege change.
- Issue a **new session id on login/re-auth** (session fixation). Use TLS everywhere; consider HSTS.
- Separate authentication from authorization; never treat “logged in” as full access.
- Store passwords with suitable KDF+salt (NIST SP 800-63B). Defend CSRF for cookie sessions; tighten CORS with credentials.

## 2. Input Validation & Attack Surface

- Trust nothing external; **server-side validation is mandatory**; client checks are UX only.
- Prefer allowlists; encode/escape for output context (HTML/URL/JS/CSS/SQL binding).
- Mitigate SQLi (parameterized/ORM), XSS (auto-escape/sanitizer/CSP), CSRF, command/path injection, SSRF (allowlist hosts).
- Uploads: size/type/magic-byte checks; non-executable storage; server-generated filenames.
- Schema-validate at boundaries; authorize per resource action (IDOR); rate-limit abuse.
- Fail closed; user-safe messages vs detailed logs; log security-relevant denials.

## 3. Secrets Management

- Never commit secrets, put them in public env prefixes, or log them.
- `.env` gitignored; `.env.example` names + dummies only.
- Load/validate config at boot (fail fast); centralize env access; fake values in fixtures.
- Use secret scanning (e.g. gitleaks) in hooks/CI; on leak, **rotate keys**, don’t only rewrite git history.
